# Target C — Section 121: exact global payment for the aggregate two-arc detour

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**  
**Status:** unreviewed proof-only research candidate.  
**Base:** `b8c040f94899b886a7183abfc5c39ae82b1c7c8a`.  
**Dependencies:** canonical Sections 62, 75, 82, 83; proof-only Section 116 and hostile-audited proof-only Section 120.

Section 120 proves that the Type-II full-core branch must pay a linear aggregate
harmonic-cell detour

\[
E_\Sigma:=\sum_r\bigl(L_r+P_{{\rm exc},r}-2d_r\bigr),
\qquad
\liminf {E_\Sigma\over D}\ge {\pi\over2}(2-\sqrt3).
\]

Its hostile audit correctly leaves one globalization problem open: different harmonic
induced graphs may reuse loaded boundary data, so the left side is not yet one
injective global boundary quantity.

This section resolves the purely combinatorial part of that payment problem. It proves
three finite statements:

1. every local strict-rhombus face is one physical global strict rhombus, and no such
   rhombus is counted by two harmonic cells;
2. a loaded-loaded contact is used by at most one harmonic cell when it is a force-hull
   side, and by at most two harmonic cells otherwise;
3. the entire aggregate detour has an exact source-level face-incidence identity.

The remaining obstruction after these reductions is geometric, not bookkeeping: one
still needs a closing upper bound on the surviving global defect currency or a
source-preserving deformation.

---

## 121.1 Notation

Use the canonical post-ZF notation. For every unloaded positive-contact component
`I_r`, let

\[
A_r\subset B,
\qquad P_r=\operatorname{conv}(A_r),
\qquad d_r=\operatorname{diam}(P_r),
\]

and let `H_r` be the induced positive-contact graph on `I_r\cup A_r`, including every
loaded-loaded unit contact whose two endpoints lie in `A_r`.

Write

- `M_r=|E(H_r)|`;
- `R_r` for the number of bounded quadrilateral faces of `H_r`;
- `L_r` for the occurrence-counted outer-face walk length;
- `P_{{\rm exc},r}` for the total boundary degree of bounded nonquadrilateral faces;
- `c_r` for the loaded-loaded contacts whose endpoints both lie in `A_r`.

Put

\[
R_{\rm loc}:=\sum_rR_r,
\qquad
C_{\rm att}:=\sum_rc_r,
\qquad
d_\Sigma:=\sum_rd_r.
\tag{121.1}
\]

For the full positive-contact core write

- `R_4` for the number of bounded strict-rhombus faces;
- `f` for the number of bounded nonquadrilateral faces;
- `e_{BB}` for the loaded-loaded contact count;
- `q=|B|`;
- `k=d_H+s_Z`.

Finally let `m_B` be the number of nonunit force-hull sides and `c_B` the number of
loaded-loaded unit contacts beyond the unit force-hull sides, so canonical Section 75
records

\[
e_{BB}=q-m_B+c_B.
\tag{121.2}
\]

---

## 121.2 A local strict rhombus is a physical global rhombus

### Lemma 121.1 — no omitted source point can lie in a strict unit rhombus

Let `R` be a unit rhombus in a unit-separated triangle-free source. Then the interior
of `R` contains no other source point.

### Proof

Let `o` be the center of `R`. Triangle-freeness and unit separation make both
diagonals strictly longer than one. Since `R` is a nondegenerate unit rhombus, both
half-diagonals are nevertheless strictly shorter than one.

Join `o` to the four vertices. This decomposes `R` into four triangles. Every such
triangle has one side equal to a side of `R`, hence of length one, and its two other
sides are half-diagonals, hence strictly shorter than one. Therefore each closed
triangle has diameter one. A point in its relative interior is at distance strictly
less than one from each endpoint of its unit side. A point in the relative interior of
a rhombus side is likewise at distance strictly less than one from a side endpoint.
Thus no distinct source point can lie in the interior of `R` or in the relative
interior of one of its sides. `square`

### Lemma 121.2 — local-to-global rhombus injection

Every quadrilateral face counted by some `R_r` is a bounded strict-rhombus face of the
full positive-contact core. Moreover the same physical rhombus is counted by at most
one index `r`.

### Proof

Canonical Section 82 classifies every quadrilateral face of `H_r` as a strict unit
rhombus. Lemma 121.1 excludes every omitted source vertex from its interior. A full
positive-contact edge cannot properly cross one of its unit sides by planarity, and
there is no omitted endpoint inside the rhombus from which an additional contact chord
could arise. Hence the same four-cycle is a face of the full positive-contact core.

Suppose one physical rhombus were counted by both `H_r` and `H_s`.

If it has an unloaded vertex, that vertex belongs to both `I_r` and `I_s`, because
unloaded vertices are never attachment vertices. Hence `r=s`.

If all four vertices are loaded, all four belong to `A_r\cap A_s`. Canonical Section
83's incidence argument forbids two distinct harmonic components from sharing three
loaded attachments: contracting the two components and retaining the six incidences
to three shared loaded vertices would violate the planar bound
`e<=q+2c-2`. Four shared attachments are therefore impossible. Again `r=s`.

Thus

\[
\boxed{R_{\rm loc}\le R_4.}
\tag{121.3}
\]

The same proof is area-preserving: if `A_{4,r}^{\rm loc}` denotes the total area of
all local strict-rhombus faces of `H_r`, then

\[
\boxed{
\sum_r A_{4,r}^{\rm loc}
\le A_4,
}
\tag{121.4}
\]

with no repeated physical rhombus. `square`

### Consequence for Section 120

Proof-only Section 116 applies to the full local strict-rhombus area, so before any
discarding one has the finite inequality

\[
\boxed{
E_\Sigma
\ge(4-2\sqrt3)
\sum_r{A_{4,r}^{\rm loc}\over d_r}.
}
\tag{121.5}
\]

Every unloaded-only global rhombus is still present in exactly one local graph by the
Section-120 hostile audit. Hence

\[
A_4^0\le\sum_rA_{4,r}^{\rm loc}\le A_4.
\tag{121.6}
\]

Thus (121.5) is a finite strengthening of Section 120, although the additional
loaded-touching area is only `O(D)` in the Type-II limit and does not by itself improve
the asymptotic coefficient.

---

## 121.3 Loaded-contact reuse is at most two, and hull-side reuse is at most one

For a loaded-loaded contact `uv`, put

\[
\nu(uv):=\#\{r:u,v\in A_r\}.
\tag{121.7}
\]

Then

\[
C_{\rm att}=\sum_{uv\in E_{BB}}\nu(uv).
\tag{121.8}
\]

### Lemma 121.3 — contact multiplicity

If `uv` is a force-hull side, then `nu(uv)<=1`. For every other loaded-loaded contact,
`nu(uv)<=2`.

### Proof

Canonical Section 83 gives pairwise interior-disjoint convex cells `P_r`.

First suppose `uv` is a side of the strictly convex force hull `F`. Its endpoints are
consecutive loaded hull vertices. Whenever `u,v\in A_r`, the segment `uv` is therefore
a side of `P_r`. All cells lie in the same closed half-plane bounded by the hull-side
line. Two positive-area convex polygons that share the full side `uv` and lie on the
same side of its line have intersecting interiors in every sufficiently small one-sided
neighborhood of a relative-interior point of `uv`. Thus two distinct cells cannot both
contain that hull side.

Now suppose `uv` is not a force-hull side. If `uv` is a diagonal of one `P_r`, its
relative interior lies in `int(P_r)`. Any second cell containing both endpoints also
contains the segment `uv`; whether that segment is a side or a diagonal of the second
cell, the two cell interiors then intersect near a relative-interior point of `uv`.
So multiplicity greater than one is possible only when `uv` is a side of every cell
that uses it. Interior-disjoint convex cells sharing one side can occupy at most one
cell on each side of the supporting line. Hence there are at most two. `square`

There are exactly `q-m_B` unit force-hull sides, while `c_B` counts the other
loaded-loaded contacts. Therefore

\[
\boxed{
C_{\rm att}
\le(q-m_B)+2c_B
=e_{BB}+c_B.
}
\tag{121.9}
\]

This is the promised global payment theorem for loaded-contact reuse.

---

## 121.4 Exact aggregate face-incidence identity

For one connected plane graph `H_r`, face-edge handshaking gives

\[
2M_r=L_r+4R_r+P_{{\rm exc},r}.
\]

Hence

\[
L_r+P_{{\rm exc},r}-2d_r
=2M_r-4R_r-2d_r.
\tag{121.10}
\]

The non-loaded-loaded positive contacts occur in exactly one harmonic induced graph,
so

\[
\sum_rM_r=e_C-e_{BB}+C_{\rm att},
\tag{121.11}
\]

where `e_C` is the full positive-contact edge count on the nonisolated core.

Canonical mixed-rigidity and the exact full face ledger give

\[
e_C=2m-2-q+k,
\qquad
m=R_4+f+q-k+1,
\]

and therefore

\[
\boxed{e_C=2R_4+2f+q-k.}
\tag{121.12}
\]

Put

\[
\Delta_R:=R_4-R_{\rm loc}\ge0.
\tag{121.13}
\]

Summing (121.10) and using (121.11)--(121.13) yields the exact finite identity

\[
\boxed{
E_\Sigma
=4\Delta_R+4f
+2\bigl(q-k-e_{BB}+C_{\rm att}\bigr)
-2d_\Sigma.
}
\tag{121.14}
\]

This equality is the source-level form of the Section-120 detour demand.

Using (121.9),

\[
\boxed{
E_\Sigma
\le4\Delta_R+4f+2(q-k+c_B)-2d_\Sigma.
}
\tag{121.15}
\]

Canonical Section 75 gives the exact identity

\[
m_B+k=c_I+c_B+s_C+2.
\tag{121.16}
\]

Eliminating `c_B` from (121.15) gives the cleaner global-payment upper bound

\[
\boxed{
E_\Sigma
\le
4\Delta_R+4f
+2(q+m_B-c_I-s_C-2)
-2d_\Sigma.
}
\tag{121.17}
\]

No harmonic-cell boundary occurrence appears on the right side. Every term is a
global source/core quantity or the sum of actual cell diameters.

---

## 121.5 Composition with the Section-120 lower bound

Combining hostile-audited proof-only Section 120 with (121.17), every Type-II survivor
sequence must satisfy

\[
\boxed{
\liminf_{D\to\infty}{1\over D}
\left[
4\Delta_R+4f
+2(q+m_B-c_I-s_C-2)
-2d_\Sigma
\right]
\ge {\pi\over2}(2-\sqrt3).
}
\tag{121.18}
\]

Equivalently,

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {1\over D}
\left[
2\Delta_R+2f+q+m_B-c_I-s_C-2-d_\Sigma
\right]
\ge {\pi\over4}(2-\sqrt3).
}
\tag{121.19}
\]

This is a necessary source-level inequality for any surviving Type-II sequence.

---

## 121.6 Hostile scope check and exact remaining obstruction

The new statements do **not** close C-II.

1. `Delta_R` is only known to be boundary order. Every unloaded-only global rhombus is
   local, so `Delta_R` is supported on loaded-touching rhombi and is `O(D)`, but the
   current coefficient is not small enough to discard it in (121.18).
2. The exceptional-face count `f` and nonunit hull-side count `m_B` are also allowed
   to be linear outside the near-endpoint slack regimes.
3. The negative term `d_Sigma` is useful but no current all-regime theorem forces it
   large enough relative to the positive defect terms.
4. Contact-isolated source points never enter the derivation. They remain in the full
   source exactly as required by the canonical normalization.
5. No KKT coefficient floor, rejected cap containment, incident-diameter Euler path,
   or full-source ZR/ZF extension is used.

The aggregate-multiplicity problem from the Section-120 audit is therefore partly
resolved: loaded contact reuse and local rhombus reuse are now paid exactly. The live
closure target is narrower:

\[
\boxed{
4\Delta_R+4f+2(q+m_B-c_I-s_C-2)-2d_\Sigma
< {\pi\over2}(2-\sqrt3)D
}
\]

from global minimality or another structural theorem would contradict Section 120.
Alternatively it is enough to derive a deformation whenever the left side has the
required positive linear size.

**Disposition:** unreviewed proof-only candidate; theorem authority unchanged.
